<Base title="Indexes" description="How a PostgreSQL B-tree index finds rows, counted in pages: index entries and TIDs, B-tree searches, range scans, the planner’s choice of scan, composite, covering, partial and expression indexes, and what each index costs a write.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header">
   <p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p>
   <h1>Indexes</h1>
   <div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational deep dive</span><span>16 min read</span><span>PostgreSQL 18</span></div>
  </header>

  <p>A <strong>B-tree index</strong>, the default kind in PostgreSQL, is a separate, sorted copy of one or more columns of a table. Each entry holds a value from those columns and the location of the row it came from. Because the entries are sorted, equal values sit together in the index, even when their rows are scattered through the table.</p>
  <p>Reading an index is extra work, and so is keeping it up to date on every write. What it can save is reading the table. So for a given query and index, how many pages does PostgreSQL have to read?</p>
  <p class="example-note">The figures use an invented table of 36 orders, six to a page, so every row stays visible. Each section then gives the same measurement on a generated table of 60,000 orders, taken in PGlite 0.5.8, a WebAssembly build of PostgreSQL 18.3. <a href="#run">Run it yourself</a> has the SQL.</p>

  <nav class="index-contents" aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#scan">Sequential scan</a></li><li><a href="#entries">Index entries and TIDs</a></li><li><a href="#btree">B-tree</a></li><li><a href="#range">Range scans</a></li><li><a href="#selectivity">Selectivity and the planner’s choice of scan</a></li><li><a href="#composite">Composite (multicolumn) indexes</a></li><li><a href="#covering">Covering indexes and index-only scans</a></li><li><a href="#kinds">Partial, expression and unique indexes</a></li><li><a href="#writes">Index write costs</a></li><li><a href="#check">Checking an index with EXPLAIN</a></li><li><a href="#run">Run it yourself</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="scan">
   <h2>Sequential scan</h2>
   <p>A <strong>sequential scan</strong> reads every page of a table in order and tests each row against the query’s conditions. PostgreSQL stores a table in 8 kB <strong>pages</strong>, in a structure called the <strong>heap</strong>, because its rows sit in no particular order. The storage article’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#pages">Pages</a> section looks inside one.</p>
   <p>Figure 1 scans the invented table for customer 42. Watch the counters: only four rows match.</p>
   <ScanFigure number={1} />
   <p>The scan read 6 pages and checked 36 rows to return 4. Its cost follows the size of the table, not the size of the answer. On the generated table, the same lookup read all 1,725 pages and discarded 59,940 rows to return 60.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="entries">
   <h2>Index entries and TIDs</h2>
   <p>An <strong>index entry</strong> pairs a key with a row location. The <strong>key</strong> is the indexed column’s value. The location is a <strong>TID</strong>, short for tuple identifier; a tuple is PostgreSQL’s word for a stored row. A TID is a page number plus an item number on that page, and PostgreSQL shows it as the hidden column <code>ctid</code>. Order 3, the third row on page 0, has TID (0,3).</p>
   <p>Figure 2 builds an index on <code>customer_id</code>. Customer 42’s rows are on four different pages. Watch where their entries go.</p>
   <BuildFigure number={2} />
   <p>After sorting, customer 42’s entries sit side by side. Finding them means finding the first 42 and reading forward until the key changes. The table itself is not reordered: the index holds the order, and the TIDs lead back to the rows.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, an index on <code>customer_id</code> cut the lookup from 1,725 pages to 62. It read 2 index pages to find the 60 entries, then the 60 table pages they point to, because customer 42’s orders sit on 60 different pages.</p>
   <p>Not every database stores a location. MySQL’s InnoDB keeps the table itself in a B-tree ordered by the primary key, and its other indexes hold each row’s primary key instead. A lookup through one of them then searches the primary key’s tree as well.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="btree">
   <h2>B-tree</h2>
   <p>So far, nothing says how a search finds the first 42. A <strong>B-tree</strong> is a tree of index pages that lets a search jump there instead of reading the entries from the start. The sorted entries live on <strong>leaf pages</strong>. Above them, <strong>branch pages</strong> hold one key for each page below, marking where that page’s range begins; PostgreSQL’s documentation calls them internal pages. The single page at the top is the <strong>root</strong>.</p>
   <p>Figure 3 draws the invented index as a B-tree on its side, with the root on the left. Pick a customer and count the index pages the search reads.</p>
   <DescentFigure number={3} />
   <p>Every search in this tree reads three index pages, whatever the key: root, branch and leaf. That count is the tree’s <strong>depth</strong>, and it grows very slowly. A real leaf page holds hundreds of entries, so each extra level multiplies the rows the tree can reach by hundreds.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="depth-caption"><table><caption id="depth-caption">B-tree depth for an index on one integer column, measured in PostgreSQL 18</caption><thead><tr><th>Rows</th><th>Levels</th><th>Index pages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100,000</td><td>2</td><td>276</td></tr><tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>3</td><td>2,745</td></tr><tr><td>10,000,000</td><td>3</td><td>27,422</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>A freshly built leaf page held 367 integer entries, and going from 1 million rows to 10 million added no level. A lookup for one key among the 10 million read 4 pages: 3 in the index and 1 in the table. The generated customer index has two levels, a root and its leaf pages, which is why its lookup read 2 index pages. It also stores each repeated key once, with a list of its 60 TIDs called a <strong>posting list</strong>.</p>
   <p>Pages on the same level are linked to their neighbours, so a scan can step from one leaf page to the next without going back up the tree. The next section uses those links.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="range">
   <h2>Range scans</h2>
   <p>A <strong>range scan</strong> descends to the first entry at or above a lower bound, then reads entries in order until it passes the upper bound. B-trees serve <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>IN</code> and <code>IS NULL</code> this way.</p>
   <p>Figure 4 runs a range scan for customers 20 to 34. Watch how many times the search descends, and what it does at the end of a leaf page.</p>
   <RangeFigure number={4} />
   <p>The search descended once, to the first 20. At the end of each leaf page it followed the link to the next, and it stopped at 42, the first key past the range. The entries came out in key order, so the <code>ORDER BY</code> needed no extra work.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, customers 40 to 45 have 360 orders, and the index search read 3 pages to collect them: the root and two leaf pages. But the plan then read the matching table pages in page order, which lost the index order, and sorted the rows again. The next section shows when PostgreSQL does that.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="selectivity">
   <h2>Selectivity and the planner’s choice of scan</h2>
   <p><strong>Selectivity</strong> is the fraction of a table’s rows that a condition matches. A condition matching 60 rows out of 60,000 is very selective; one matching half the table is not. The <strong>planner</strong>, the part of PostgreSQL that chooses how to run a query, estimates selectivity from statistics that the <code>ANALYZE</code> command collects. Then it picks one of three ways to fetch the rows:</p>
   <ul>
    <li>An <strong>index scan</strong> follows each entry to its row, in index order.</li>
    <li>A <strong>bitmap heap scan</strong> collects the matching TIDs from the index, sorts them by page, then reads each table page once. It saves repeat visits but loses the index order.</li>
    <li>A <strong>sequential scan</strong> ignores the index and reads every page.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>Figure 5 widens a range one customer at a time. Watch how quickly the matching rows spread across the pages.</p>
   <SelectivityFigure number={5} />
   <p>By the third step, with 8 rows or 22% of the table matched, every page held a match. From there the index can only add work. A bitmap heap scan reads all six pages anyway, and an index scan reads some of them more than once.</p>
   <p>The order of reads matters too. A sequential scan reads pages one after another, which storage serves quickly, while index lookups jump around. The planner prices a jumping read at four times a sequential one, through the settings <code>random_page_cost</code> (default 4) and <code>seq_page_cost</code> (default 1). The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-query.html#GUC-RANDOM-PAGE-COST">documentation</a> suggests lowering <code>random_page_cost</code> on SSDs or when the data is mostly in memory.</p>
   <p>The planner works from those estimates, not from counted pages. On the generated table, with only the customer index, it chose a bitmap heap scan up to <code>customer_id &lt;= 500</code>, half the table, and a sequential scan from 550. At 500 the bitmap scan read 922 of the 1,725 pages, because the generator puts runs of consecutive customers on each page.</p>
   <p>The planner’s statistics don’t describe that layout. A copy of the table with its rows shuffled, where the same condition touched 1,715 pages, switched at the same point. The choice came from cost estimates. The sequential scan’s estimate stays at 2,475 whatever the bound, while the bitmap scan’s grows with the number of matching rows: just under 2,475 at 500, just over it at 550.</p>
   <p>Once every page holds a match, the index can’t save a read. The planner may switch earlier or later than that point, because it estimates rather than counts. When the choice matters, compare the pages each plan actually read.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="composite">
   <h2>Composite (multicolumn) indexes</h2>
   <p>A <strong>composite index</strong> has more than one key column. Its entries sort by the first column, then by the second among entries that tie on the first, and so on. The column order decides which entries sit together.</p>
   <p>Figure 6 asks for customer 42’s two newest orders since 10 January. Switch between the three indexes and count the entries each one reads.</p>
   <CompositeFigure number={6} />
   <p>With <code>customer_id</code> alone, the index found customer 42’s four orders but knew nothing about their dates, so every row was fetched, checked and sorted. With <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC)</code>, the entries were already newest first, and the scan stopped after two. With the date first, every customer’s entries were interleaved by date, and the scan read 16 to find two.</p>
   <p>The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-multicolumn.html">documentation</a> gives the rule. Equality conditions on the leading columns, plus a range condition on the next one, limit how much of the index is read. PostgreSQL 18 can also skip through an index with no condition on its leading column, one search per distinct value, but that pays only when the column has few distinct values. Put the columns compared with <code>=</code> first, then the column used for the range or the sort.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, the <strong>history query</strong> asks for customer 42’s ten newest orders since 1 February:</p>
   <Sql code={history} label="The history query: customer 42’s ten newest orders since 1 February" />
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="history-caption"><table><caption id="history-caption">The history query on the generated table with each index in place</caption><thead><tr><th>Index</th><th>Plan</th><th>Pages read</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>None</td><td>Sequential scan, then sort</td><td>1,725</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(customer_id)</code></td><td>Bitmap heap scan: 60 rows fetched, 45 rejected, then sort</td><td>62</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(placed_at DESC, customer_id)</code></td><td>Index scan through about 11,000 entries, then a partial sort</td><td>57</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code></td><td>Index scan, stops after 10 rows, no sort</td><td>13</td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <p>The reversed index found the right ten rows only after reading about 11,000 entries for every customer, newest first. The composite index read 13 pages: 3 index pages, one per level, and 10 table pages, one per row. Its entries are wider than the customer index’s, so its tree has three levels.</p>
   <p>The <code>order_id DESC</code> column breaks ties between orders placed in the same instant, as the query’s <code>ORDER BY</code> does. Keep the <code>ORDER BY</code> even when an index supplies the order, because SQL guarantees no order without it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="covering">
   <h2>Covering indexes and index-only scans</h2>
   <p>An <strong>index-only scan</strong> answers a query from the index without visiting the table. It needs two things. Every column the query uses must be in the index, and PostgreSQL must know each row is visible without checking the table.</p>
   <p>The first is about columns. The history query returns <code>total</code>, which the composite index lacks, so each of its ten rows needs a table visit. <code>INCLUDE</code> adds columns to every entry as stored values that are not part of the sort. An index holding every column a query needs is a <strong>covering index</strong> for that query.</p>
   <p>The second comes from how PostgreSQL handles concurrent changes. An update writes a new version of the row and keeps the old one for transactions that started earlier, and an index entry doesn’t record which transactions can see its row. <code>VACUUM</code>, PostgreSQL’s cleanup of row versions no transaction needs, is what records it. The storage article’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#vacuum">VACUUM and HOT updates</a> section follows those versions.</p>
   <p>Figure 7 shows one leaf page of the composite index beside the table. Include <code>total</code> first, then run VACUUM, and watch the heap-fetches counter.</p>
   <CoveringFigure number={7} />
   <p>Each ticked box is one bit in the <strong>visibility map</strong>. VACUUM sets it when every row on the page is visible to everyone, and any change clears it. With <code>total</code> included but no bits set, the scan still visited each row’s page to check it.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, the covering index first gave an <code>Index Only Scan</code> with <code>Heap Fetches: 10</code> and 13 pages read, as before. The table was freshly loaded, so no bits were set. After <code>VACUUM</code>, it reported <code>Heap Fetches: 0</code> and read 4 pages.</p>
   <p>Including <code>total</code> made the index 2.9 MiB instead of 2.3 MiB, beside a 13.5 MiB table. An included column is still part of the index, so changing <code>total</code> now means changing the index too. A table that changes quickly also keeps clearing its bits. A covering index pays for a frequent query on a table that changes slowly. On a busy table, check <code>Heap Fetches</code> before relying on it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="kinds">
   <h2>Partial, expression and unique indexes</h2>
   <p>A <strong>partial index</strong> holds entries only for rows that match its own <code>WHERE</code> clause. A fifth of the generated orders are pending:</p>
   <Sql code={partialIndex} label="Index only the pending orders" />
   <p>This index is 35 pages, against 166 for an index on <code>placed_at</code> covering every row, and inserts of paid orders skip it. A query for the ten newest pending orders read it backwards and touched 4 pages. The planner uses a partial index only when the query’s own conditions imply the index’s <code>WHERE</code> clause.</p>
   <p>An <strong>expression index</strong> indexes the result of an expression instead of a column. A report of one day’s orders can’t use an index on <code>placed_at</code> through <code>placed_at::date</code>, and that cast can’t be indexed either, because its result depends on the session’s time zone. A fixed time zone makes it indexable:</p>
   <Sql code={dayIndex} label="Index each order’s date in UTC" />
   <p>A query that repeats the expression exactly, <code>(placed_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date = date '2026-02-01'</code>, used the index and touched 47 pages to count 1,440 orders. The same question written as <code>placed_at::date = date '2026-02-01'</code> read all 1,725 pages. The planner matches the expression as written.</p>
   <p>A <strong>unique index</strong> also refuses a second entry with the same key. PostgreSQL creates one for every primary key and <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint, so <code>index_orders_pkey</code> is one. An index on <code>(customer_id, placed_at)</code> declared <code>UNIQUE</code> would refuse two orders for one customer in the same instant.</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL doesn’t index the referencing column of a foreign key automatically. If <code>customer_id</code> referenced a customers table, lookups by customer and deletes of customers would both need an index on it. For other index types, such as GIN, GiST and BRIN, see <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/">Indexes in PostgreSQL</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="writes">
   <h2>Index write costs</h2>
   <p>Each index is another copy of some of the table’s data, so a write to the table is also a write to its indexes. Figure 8 follows one insert and one update through a table with up to four indexes: the primary key, <code>customer_id</code>, <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC)</code> and <code>total</code>. Change the number of indexes and count the pages each statement writes.</p>
   <WriteFigure number={8} />
   <p>With four indexes, the insert wrote 8 pages, four of them for one page split. With only the primary key, it wrote 2. The update wrote 1 page until an index held <code>total</code>, then 5.</p>
   <p>On the generated data, with just the primary key, inserting 10,000 orders wrote 3.3 MB of write-ahead log, the record PostgreSQL keeps for crash recovery. With four more indexes, the same insert wrote 7.7 MB.</p>

   <h3>Page splits</h3>
   <p>When the leaf page where an entry belongs is full, PostgreSQL first tries to make room. It removes entries for row versions no transaction can see any more, or merges duplicate keys into a posting list. If that fails, the page <strong>splits</strong>. Half its entries move to a new page, the next leaf page’s link is updated, and the branch page above gains a pointer. A split can travel upwards when the branch page is full too, and a split of the root adds a level to the tree.</p>

   <h3>HOT updates</h3>
   <p>An update writes a new row version. If no indexed column changed and the new version fits on the same page, the update is a <strong>heap-only tuple</strong> (HOT) update, and no index is touched. The storage article’s <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/#vacuum">VACUUM and HOT updates</a> section shows how the old entries still lead to the row. Change a column any index holds, including an <code>INCLUDE</code> column, and every index on the table gets a new entry.</p>
   <p>On the generated data, updating <code>total</code> on 600 rows gave 600 HOT updates when no index held <code>total</code>, and none once the covering index included it. That table left a tenth of each page free, with <code>fillfactor = 90</code>. With the default fillfactor of 100, the freshly loaded pages had no room, and none of the 600 updates was HOT.</p>

   <h3>Building an index (CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY)</h3>
   <p>A plain <code>CREATE INDEX</code> blocks writes to the table until it finishes. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html#SQL-CREATEINDEX-CONCURRENTLY"><code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code></a> allows writes during the build but takes longer. If the build fails, it leaves an invalid index behind. Drop it, or rebuild it with <code>REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>.</p>

   <h3>Finding unused indexes</h3>
   <p>An index no query reads still costs every write. The <code>pg_stat_user_indexes</code> view counts each index’s scans:</p>
   <Sql code={usage} label="Count how often each index on a table has been scanned" />
   <p>An index whose <code>idx_scan</code> stays at zero over a representative period is a candidate for removal. Each server keeps its own counts, so check every replica too, and make sure the counters haven’t been reset recently. An index that enforces a primary key or unique constraint stays, whatever its count.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="check">
   <h2>Checking an index with EXPLAIN</h2>
   <p><code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the plan PostgreSQL chose for a query. <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> also runs the query and reports what each step did and how many pages it touched. Here <code>ANALYZE</code> means run and measure; the separate <code>ANALYZE</code> command collects the planner’s statistics. Since PostgreSQL 18, <code>ANALYZE</code> reports pages without <code>BUFFERS</code>, but older versions need it.</p>
   <Sql code={explain(history)} label="Run the history query and report its plan" />
   <p>With the covering index in place and the table vacuumed, the important part of the plan looks like this, simplified: costs, timings, a type cast and one detail line are left out, and long lines are wrapped.</p>
   <pre class="plan"><code>{plan}</code></pre>
   <p>Read it from the most indented line outwards. The index-only scan produces rows in order, and <code>Limit</code> stops asking for more after ten. Four parts of a plan tell you what an index did:</p>
   <ul>
    <li><strong>The node type.</strong> <code>Seq Scan</code>, <code>Index Scan</code>, <code>Index Only Scan</code> or <code>Bitmap Heap Scan</code> names the route.</li>
    <li><strong><code>Index Cond</code> against <code>Filter</code>.</strong> Conditions under <code>Index Cond</code> were used in the index search. Conditions under <code>Filter</code> were checked on rows after fetching them, and <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code> counts the wasted fetches.</li>
    <li><strong><code>Heap Fetches</code>.</strong> In an index-only scan, the rows that still needed a table visit.</li>
    <li><strong><code>Buffers</code>.</strong> The <code>shared hit</code> and <code>read</code> counts are pages found in memory and pages read from storage. A temporary table, like this one, reports them under <code>local</code> instead.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>A paid-only version of the history query produces a <code>Filter</code>:</p>
   <Sql code={residual} label="Ask for customer 42’s ten newest paid orders" />
   <p>The index has no <code>status</code>, so PostgreSQL fetched 12 rows and discarded 2 pending ones to return 10. For a customer whose recent orders were mostly pending, the scan would read much further. An index on <code>(customer_id, status, placed_at DESC)</code> would move the condition into <code>Index Cond</code>, at the cost of splitting each customer’s history by status.</p>
   <p>An index is doing its job when the plan shows your condition under <code>Index Cond</code>, not <code>Filter</code>, and <code>Heap Fetches</code> near zero.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="run">
   <h2>Run it yourself</h2>
   <p>Every measurement in this article comes from one script. The setup creates a temporary table of 60,000 orders for 1,000 customers, 60 orders each, with a long delivery note so that the table fills 1,725 pages. It disappears when the session ends.</p>
   <Sql code={setupText} label="Create and fill a temporary table of 60,000 generated orders" />
   <p>Then <a href={experimentUrl} download="index-experiment.sql">download the complete experiment</a> and run it in a fresh session with <code>psql -f index-experiment.sql</code>. In a query editor, run it statement by statement with autocommit on, because <code>VACUUM</code> can’t run inside a transaction block. A server may report slightly different page counts than PGlite did, and none of the numbers are timings.</p>
   <details class="run-steps"><summary>Every step after the setup, in article order</summary><Sql code={steps} label="The experiment after the setup" /></details>
   <p class="example-note">The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=index-order">index-order lesson</a> runs the history query in your browser, and the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=index-selectivity">index-selectivity lesson</a> widens a filter until the planner stops using the index.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="essay-sources"><h2>Sources and further reading</h2>
   <p>Checked on 16 October 2026 against the PostgreSQL 18 documentation. The 36-order table is invented; the measurements come from PGlite 0.5.8 (PostgreSQL 18.3).</p>
   <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/btree.html">B-Tree Indexes</a>: page structure, deduplication into posting lists, and bottom-up deletion.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-multicolumn.html">Multicolumn Indexes</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-index-only-scans.html">Index-Only Scans and Covering Indexes</a>: column order, skip scan, <code>INCLUDE</code> and the visibility map.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-partial.html">Partial Indexes</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-expressional.html">Indexes on Expressions</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-unique.html">Unique Indexes</a>.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-hot.html">Heap-Only Tuples (HOT)</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html">CREATE INDEX</a>, including <code>CONCURRENTLY</code>.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-query.html">Query Planning</a>: <code>random_page_cost</code> and <code>seq_page_cost</code>. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/using-explain.html">Using EXPLAIN</a>: estimated and actual rows, and buffer counts.</li>
    <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">Storage and durability</a>: pages, row versions, VACUUM and HOT updates.</li>
    <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">How a query runs</a>: how PostgreSQL combines scans, joins and sorting.</li>
    <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/indexes/">Indexes in PostgreSQL</a>: B-tree, GIN and BRIN compared, with operator support and physical correlation.</li>
   </ul>
  </section>
